NNAAMMEE
     sseettppggiidd, sseettppggrrpp — set process group

LLIIBBRRAARRYY
     Standard C Library (libc, -lc)

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ##iinncclluuddee <<uunniissttdd..hh>>

     _i_n_t
     sseettppggiidd(_p_i_d___t _p_i_d, _p_i_d___t _p_g_r_p);

     _i_n_t
     sseettppggrrpp(_p_i_d___t _p_i_d, _p_i_d___t _p_g_r_p);

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The sseettppggiidd() system call sets the process group of the specified process
     _p_i_d to the specified _p_g_r_p.  If _p_i_d is zero, then the call applies to the
     current process.  If _p_g_r_p is zero, then the process id of the process
     specified by _p_i_d is used instead.

     If the affected process is not the invoking process, then it must be a
     child of the invoking process, it must not have performed an exec(3) op‐
     eration, and both processes must be in the same session.  The requested
     process group ID must already exist in the session of the caller, or it
     must be equal to the target process ID.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The sseettppggiidd() function returns the value 0 if successful; otherwise the
     value -1 is returned and the global variable _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the
     error.

CCOOMMPPAATTIIBBIILLIITTYY
     The sseettppggrrpp() system call is identical to sseettppggiidd(), and is retained for
     calling convention compatibility with historical versions of BSD.

EERRRROORRSS
     The sseettppggiidd() system call will fail and the process group will not be al‐
     tered if:

     [EINVAL]           The requested process group ID is not legal.

     [ESRCH]            The requested process does not exist.

     [ESRCH]            The target process is not the calling process or a
                        child of the calling process.

     [EACCES]           The requested process is a child of the calling
                        process, but it has performed an exec(3) operation.

     [EPERM]            The target process is a session leader.

     [EPERM]            The requested process group ID is not in the session
                        of the caller, and it is not equal to the process ID
                        of the target process.

